  <!-- WHAT IF FAULT FOUND -->
  <section class="pf-section" style="background:#f8fafc;">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>What Happens If the Inspection Finds a Problem?</h2>
      <p class="pf-sub">We follow the Gas Industry Unsafe Situations Procedure on every St Helens inspection. Here is exactly what happens if a fault is found.</p>
      <div class="pf-who-grid" style="margin-top:2rem;">
        <div class="pf-who-item" style="border-left:3px solid #dc2626;">
          <div style="background:#dc2626;color:#ffffff;font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-weight:900;font-size:1rem;min-width:1.75rem;height:1.75rem;border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;">1</div>
          <div><strong>Immediately Dangerous</strong><p>If an appliance poses an immediate risk, our engineer turns it off, disconnects the gas supply and places a warning notice. We explain and give a written repair quote before leaving. A CP12 cannot be issued until the fault is rectified.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-who-item" style="border-left:3px solid #d97706;">
          <div style="background:#d97706;color:#ffffff;font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-weight:900;font-size:1rem;min-width:1.75rem;height:1.75rem;border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;">2</div>
          <div><strong>At Risk</strong><p>If an appliance has a fault that could become dangerous but is not immediately so, we advise and give a written repair quote. We strongly recommend addressing At Risk faults before the next tenancy. A CP12 can be issued noting the fault.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-who-item" style="border-left:3px solid #16a34a;">
          <div style="background:#16a34a;color:#ffffff;font-family:'Barlow Condensed',sans-serif;font-weight:900;font-size:1rem;min-width:1.75rem;height:1.75rem;border-radius:50%;display:flex;align-items:center;justify-content:center;flex-shrink:0;">3</div>
          <div><strong>Not to Current Standard</strong><p>Older appliances that meet the standard at installation but not current standards are noted on the CP12 with a recommendation. The appliance passes but the issue is documented.</p></div>
        </div>
        <div class="pf-who-item pf-who-yes">
          <span class="pf-who-icon">✓</span>
          <div><strong>Written quote before any repair work</strong><p>If a repair is needed, a written quote is provided before any additional work starts. We never begin repair work without your approval. Straightforward repairs can often be completed on the same visit.</p></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- FIRST-TIME LET SECTION -->
  <section class="pf-section" style="background:#f1f5f9;">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>First-Time Landlords in St Helens. What You Need Before Your Tenant Moves In.</h2>
      <p class="pf-sub">St Helens has a growing buy-to-let market, particularly in WA10 and WA12. If you are renting out a property for the first time, here is what you need before any tenant can move in.</p>
      <div class="pf-card pf-card-orange" style="margin-top:2rem;">
        <h3>Before Your First Tenant. Your CP12 Checklist.</h3>
        <p>A CP12 gas safety certificate is required before any tenant moves in. The new tenant must receive a copy on or before the tenancy start date, not within 28 days as for renewals, but before day one. If your property has a gas fire, back boiler or gas hob alongside the boiler, all of these must be on the certificate. A certificate that only covers the boiler is not sufficient.</p>
        <p>For first-time St Helens landlords who have inherited a property or are renting out a home they previously lived in, the gas fire in the living room is the most commonly missed appliance. Properties in WA10, WA9 and WA11 built before 1990 frequently have gas fires that were part of the original fitting. We cover the boiler, gas cooker and hob, and email the certificate the same day. If there is a gas fire, James can disconnect and cap it safely. If you want to keep using it, it must be checked by an engineer registered for gas fires.</p>
